					<h1>Honorable Mention Winner</h1>
					<ul>
						<li><strong>Name:</strong> Ali</li>
						<li><strong>Age:</strong> 13</li>
						<li><strong>Award:</strong> A Mia doll and her book</li>
						<li><strong>Nominated by:</strong> Her mother, Dori-Ann</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div id="essay">
					<h2>A dancer with desire</h2>
					<p>Ali&#8217;s grandmother calls her &#8220;Tappy Toes&#8221; because Ali dances seven days a week, and it is the one thing that she truly loves. She has been a dancer for 11 years, competing the last six years with a dance company.</p>
					<p class="pullquote">Whatever her heart&#8217;s desire, Ali will get there because she is so dedicated to reaching her goals.</p>
					<p>Dancing is a very competitive field with lots of rejection. This has been hard for Ali to understand and accept, but as she matures, she&#8217;s been responding in an amazing way. She has learned that while she may not be what the choreographer is looking for, she is still a good dancer. This year she auditioned for performing arts high schools and didn&#8217;t get call-backs for some of them. Ali smiled and moved on.</p>
					<p>Ali has also had to learn what it feels like to not be able to dance. Ali had a torn ligament injury that kept her from dancing for three months. She tried to go to practice and watch, but it became so hard for her that instead she focused on her physical therapy so that she could get back to dance herself.</p>
					<p>Once Ali was able to perform again, she had to regain her strength and learn all ten dance numbers so that she could compete with the team. She put all of her heart into it and asked for extra help from her teachers when she was having trouble with the choreography. She never gave up and was able to compete when the team was ready, earning her the Perseverance Award from her company&#8217;s director.</p>
					<p class="last">Whatever her heart&#8217;s desire, Ali will get there because she is so dedicated to reaching her goals.</p>
				</div>
